I did not "fix" this. I measured it. Running one call per fresh process, [38;5;10mc2GJK[0m's frame lands on stack pages the process has never written, which the kernel supplies zero-filled, so the poly proxy reads back as all zeros. Two candidate C variants tested against the original: a zero-filled proxy matched [1m160/160[22m, while upstream cute_c2's "correct" poly fill matched only 31/160. So [38;5;10mc2GJK[0m zero-initializes its proxies and [38;5;10mc2MakeProxy[0m keeps its no-op poly arm — reproducing the observed behavior rather than the intended behavior. Other deliberate infidelity-preserving details are commented in the source: [38;5;10mptr_from_parts[0m falling off the end for [38;5;10mPOLY[0m, the leaked [38;5;10mmalloc[0ms, [38;5;10mc2AABBtoCapsuleManifold[0m negating [38;5;10mm->n[0m even when no manifold was produced, and the [38;5;10mx < 0 ? -x : x[0m idioms that pass [38;5;10m-0.0[0m through unchanged (verified: [38;5;10mn=(1,-0)[0m reproduces exactly).[0m[0m
